<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-section-counting-harder" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>More Counting Practice</title>

<introduction>
 <p> 
    The problems below will provide more practice with the basic counting techniques (multiplication principle, permutation, combination), and you will also practice:   
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Using the multinomial coefficient.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
         Combining counting techniques.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Thinking about more complex scenarios and events.
        </p>
      </li>
        <li>
        <p>
          Using counting techniques to help compute probabilities.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</introduction>




 <exercises xml:id="hw-counting-harder" >
   <title>Interactive Exercises</title>



   <exercise label="counting-harder-interview-coding-challenge">
    <title>Technical Interview</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            A computer science student prepares for a technical interview by studying fourteen provided coding challenges. The student was able to complete eight of the challenges. During the interview, six of the coding challenges are selected to test the student's knowledge.
            
        </p>
        <p>
            Match each block on the left with its correct contextual meaning on the right. Ultimately, you are trying to find the probability that the student can complete four of the six coding challenges selected during their interview.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
        <p>
            Think of the 14 problems as being split into two sets - those the student could complete and those that they had not mastered yet.
        </p>
    </hint>
    
    <cardsort>
        <!-- Pair 1 -->
        <match>
            <premise><m>\binom{14}{6} = 3,003</m></premise>
            <response>The total number of ways that six questions can be selected for the student's interview.</response>
        </match>
        
        <!-- Pair 2 -->
        <match>
            <premise><m>\binom{8}{4} = 70</m></premise>
            <response>The number of ways that 4 of the coding challenges that the student completed can be selected for the student's interview. </response>
        </match>
        
        <!-- Pair 3 -->
        <match>
            <premise><m>\binom{6}{2} = 15</m></premise>
            <response>The number of ways that 2 of the coding challenges that the student had not completed can be selected for the student's interview.</response>
        </match>
        
        <!-- Pair 4 -->
        <match>
            <premise><m>70 \times 15 = 1,050</m></premise>
            <response>The total number of ways that six problems can be selected for the interview where the student can complete four of them.</response>
        </match>
        
        <!-- Pair 5 -->
        <match>
            <premise><m>\frac{1050}{3003} = \frac{50}{143}</m></premise>
            <response>The probability that the student can complete four of the six coding challenges selected during their interview.</response>
        </match>
    </cardsort>

   
</exercise>

    <!-- Visual Divider ############################################################################################### -->
   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->

<exercise xml:id="counting-harder-housing-multinomial">
    <title>Campus Housing Room Assignments</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            A group of <m>12</m> college students are selected to live on a specialized floor in a residence hall. The floor has exactly <m>6</m> rooms available that must be filled: one triple room (<m>3</m> students), three double rooms (<m>2</m> students each), and three single rooms (<m>1</m> student each). 
        </p>
        <p>
            If the housing department assigns the <m>12</m> students completely at random to fill these rooms, how many distinct ways can the students be assigned to rooms?
        </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="yes" >
        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
                <m>\frac{12!}{3!\cdot  2!\cdot   2!\cdot   2!\cdot   1!\cdot   1!\cdot   1!} </m> ways
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Correct! Because the rooms themselves are distinct entities, we use the multinomial coefficient to partition the 12 individuals into groups of sizes 3, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1, and 1.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
                <m>\binom{12}{3\  2\  2\  2\  1\  1\  1} </m> 
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Correct! Because the rooms themselves are distinct entities, we use the multinomial coefficient to partition the 12 individuals into groups of sizes 3, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1, and 1.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
         <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
                <m>\binom{12}{3}\times \binom{9}{2}\times \binom{7}{2} \times \binom{5}{2} \times \binom{3}{1}  \times \binom{2}{1}  \times 1</m> 
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Correct! Because the rooms themselves are distinct entities, but order does not matter within rooms, we can use combinations to partition the 12 individuals into groups of sizes 3, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1, and 1. This calculation is equivalent to using the multinomial coefficient.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>12! </m> 
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This answer over counts, because rearranging students <em>within</em> the same room does not create a new room assignment configuration.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>\binom{12}{3} \times \binom{9}{6} \times \binom{3}{3} </m>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This calculation treats the three double rooms as a single giant group and the three single rooms as a single giant group, rather than distributing students into separate rooms.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>\binom{12}{3} \times \binom{9}{2} \times \binom{7}{1} </m> 
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This calculation only assigns students to the triple room, one double room, and one single room. The other rooms are not accounted for.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>\frac{12!}{3!\cdot   3!\cdot   3!} </m> 
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This answer attempts to use the multinomial coefficient, but the  numbers in the denominator have been chosen incorrectly.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
    </choices>

    <hint>
        <p>
            Think about partitioning a single pool of <m>12</m> distinct people into multiple specific groups. The group sizes are defined by the room capacities.
        </p>
    </hint>

   <!-- <solution>
        <p>
            This problem requires partitioning a set of distinct elements into multiple groups where the order within each group does not matter, but the groups themselves are distinct. This is calculated using the multinomial coefficient formula:
            <me>\binom{n}{n_1, n_2, n_3, \dots, n_k} = \frac{n!}{n_1! \times n_2! \times n_3! \dots n_k!}</me>
        </p>
        <p>
            1. Total number of students to assign: <m>n = 12</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            2. Identify the target group (room) sizes from the problem description:
            <ul>
                <li><p>One triple room: <m>3</m> students</p></li>
                <li><p>Three double rooms: <m>2</m> students, <m>2</m> students, and <m>2</m> students</p></li>
                <li><p>Three single rooms: <m>1</m> student, <m>1</m> student, and <m>1</m> student</p></li>
            </ul>
            Note that <m>3 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 12</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            3. Apply the multinomial formula directly:
            <me>\frac{12!}{3! \times 2! \times 2! \times 2! \times 1! \times 1! \times 1!}</me>
        </p>
        <p>
            4. Evaluate the factorials to find the final total:
            <me>\frac{479,001,600}{6 \times 2 \times 2 \times 2 \times 1 \times 1 \times 1} = \frac{479,001,600}{48} = 1,663,200</me>
        </p>
    </solution> -->
</exercise>

