<exercise label="ex-interactive-poker-prob" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Poker Hands</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            A standard deck of 52 cards features 13 distinct numerical ranks (Ace, 2, 3,...,10, Jack, Queen, King) in each of 4 distinct suits (Hearts, Diamonds, Clubs, and Spades).  
            </p>
            <p>In a standard 5-card poker match, a player is dealt a hand of 5 cards completely at random from the deck of 52. 
        </p>

        <image source="Images/Deck-of-Cards.png" width="60%" decorative="yes"/>

            
    
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Three of a Kind Calculation -->
    <task xml:id="task-poker-three-kind">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The poker hand "Three of a Kind" contains exactly 3 cards of the same rank, and 2 cards that each possess different, non-matching ranks (for example: three 10's, one 4, and one 7). 
            </p>
            <p>
                Compute the  probability of being dealt a Three of a Kind. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to four places.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin  answer="0.0211" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                    <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Correct! 
                To find the number of possible Three of a Kind hands, we complete a sequence of independent selection steps:
                </p>
                <ol marker="i">
                    <li><p>Choose 1 rank out of the 13 available to serve as the triple: <m>\binom{13}{1}</m></p></li>
                    <li><p>Choose 3 of the 4 cards of that rank that are available (to complete the selection of the triple): <m>\binom{4}{3}</m></p></li>
                    <li><p>Choose 2 distinct ranks out of the remaining 12 options for the two remaining cards: <m>\binom{12}{2}</m></p></li>
                    <li><p>Choose 1 specific card out of the 4 available suits for each of those 2 separate ranks: <m>\binom{4}{1} \cdot \binom{4}{1}</m></p></li>
                </ol>
                <p>
                Multiplying these combinations together gives us the total number of Three of a Kind hands.
                </p>
                <p>
                We then divide this by the total number of possible 5 cards hands: <m>\binom{52}{5}</m>
                        </p>
                    </feedback>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>

    </task>

  
   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->
    <!-- Part B: High Card Strategy -->
    <!-- Part B: High Card Strategy (Converted to Multiple Choice) -->
    <task xml:id="task-poker-high-card-mc">
        <statement>
           <p>
            What would be your strategy for computing the probability of not being dealt any of the poker hands? This is also referred to as a “High Card” hand.
            </p>
            <p>
                Select the equation that correctly represents the strategic approach for computing the number of possible High Card hands?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Think about what is or is not being selected with each step of the answers. Could you get one of the Poker hands with the selection?
            </p>
        </hint>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m> \binom{13}{5} \cdot 4^5</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This expression counts the number of ways to get a hand containing cards of 5 completely different ranks, each assigned a random suit. This method would count "Straights" (5 consecutive ranks) and "Flushes" (all 5 cards sharing the same suit), which cannot be part of a High Card hand.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p><m> \left[ \binom{13}{5} - 10 \right] \cdot (4^5 - 4)</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! One way to build a High Card hand is to pick 5 distinct ranks out of 13, but you must subtract off the 10 possible rank sequences that form a Straight. Then, you assign suits to those cards, but you must subtract the 4 suit configurations that would result in a flush (all cards share the same suit).</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m> \binom{13}{5} \cdot4^5 - 40</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This expression counts the number of ways to get a hand containing cards of 5 completely different ranks, each assigned a random suit. Then this method tries to handle the overlap with Straights and Flushes by subtracting a constant of 40. However, this only removes the Straight Flushes, rather than all Straights and all Flushes.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>\binom{13}{1}\binom{4}{1} \cdot \binom{12}{1}\binom{4}{1} \cdot \binom{11}{1}\binom{4}{1} \cdot \binom{10}{1}\binom{4}{1} \cdot \binom{9}{1}\binom{4}{1}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This expression tracks the dealt order of the cards. Because poker hands are unordered, this counts identical hands multiple times and fails to filter out Straights and Flushes.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </task>
</exercise>

    <!-- Visual Divider ############################################################################################### -->
   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->

<exercise label="ex-interactive-poker-joker-prob" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Poker Hands with Jokers</title>
    <introduction>
        <p> Refer to the previous problem: Poker Hands. </p>
<p>
    
 Suppose that two Jokers are added to the deck, bringing the total deck size to 54 cards. These are wild cards and can count as any rank or suit.</p>
            
            <p> Answer the questions below to find the probability of being dealt a "Three of a Kind" in this new scenario, where your hand could include 0, 1, or 2 Jokers. 
        </p>

    </introduction>

    <!-- Part C: 1 Joker Counting (Short Answer Entry) -->
    <task xml:id="task-poker-one-joker-count">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Calculate the  number of 5-card hands that contain exactly 1 Joker and form a Three of a Kind.
                </p>

                <p>
                 Note: If a Joker can optimize a hand into a higher category (like a Full House or Four of a Kind), it must. 
            </p>
                    </statement>
    
<hint>
    <p>
                To avoid completing a better hand, the 4 non-Joker cards must consist of one natural pair and two cards that each possess different, non-matching ranks.
            </p>
            </hint>
            

        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice><statement><p><m>\binom{13}{1}\binom{4}{3}\binom{12}{2}4^2\binom{2}{1} = 109,824</m> hands</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This expression gives us a triple in the four non-Joker cards. If you add a wild Joker, you end up with a "Four of a Kind." </p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m>\binom{13}{1}\binom{4}{2}\binom{12}{2}4^2\binom{2}{1} = 164,736</m> hands</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! To form  a Three of a Kind with 1 Joker, the other 4 cards must consist of a natural pair and two separate singletons of unique ranks (e.g., 10-10-4-7 + Joker). The calculation evaluates as follows:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Choose 1 rank out of 13 for the pair: <m>\binom{13}{1} </m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Choose 2 of 4 cards available of that rank (to complete the selection of the pair): <m>\binom{4}{2} </m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Choose 2 unique ranks out of the remaining 12 for reamining non-Joker cards: <m>\binom{12}{2} </m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Choose 1 suit out of 4 for each singleton: <m>4^2 </m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Choose 1 Joker out of the 2 available in the deck: <m>\binom{2}{1} = 2</m></p></li>
                    </ul>
                    
                </feedback>
                </choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>\binom{13}{1}\binom{4}{2}\binom{12}{1}\binom{4}{2}\binom{2}{1} = 5,616</m> hands</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This expression counts the number of hands  consisting of two  pairs and 1 Joker (e.g., 8-8-3-3 + Joker). The Joker turns this hand into a "Full House" instead of a Three of a Kind.</p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>\binom{13}{4}4^4\binom{2}{1} = 366,080</m> hands</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This expression counts the number of hands where the 4 non-Joker cards all have a  unique rank (e.g., A-K-Q-5 + Joker). The Joker turns this hand into a "One Pair" rather than a Three of a Kind.</p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>\binom{52}{4}\binom{2}{1} = 541,450</m> hands</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This broadly counts every 5-card hand that contains exactly 1 Joker. It  ignores what poker hand combination those cards actually form.</p>
                </feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </task>


   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->
    <!-- Part D: 2 Joker Counting (Short Answer Entry) -->
    <task xml:id="task-poker-two-joker-count">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Calculate the  number of 5-card hands that contain exactly 2 Jokers and form a Three of a Kind. Enter your answer as a whole number.
            </p>

            <p>
                Number of hands = <fillin  answer="54912" width="8"/> hands
            </p>
        </statement>
<hint>
                    <p>
                Hint 1: Think about the  3 non-Joker cards. What must they be for the two Jokers to force the hand into a "Three of a Kind"? </p>
<p>                Hint 2: Think about whether more than one Three-of-Kind can be created within the 5-card hand. If this is possible, should you include each possibility in your count? 
            </p>
            </hint>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                   <numcmp use-answer="yes"/> 
                   <feedback>
                    <p>
Correct!
                To form a Three of a Kind with exactly 2 Jokers, the remaining 3 non-Joker cards must all have different ranks (e.g., K-7-5 + 2 Jokers). The breakdown of the calculation is as follows:
                <ol marker="1">
                    <li><p>Choose 3 unique ranks out of 13 for the non-Joker cards: <m>\binom{13}{3} </m></p></li>
                    <li><p>Choose 1 suit out of 4 for each of those 3 distinct cards: <m>4^3 </m></p></li>
                    <li><p>Select both wild Jokers out of the deck: <m>\binom{2}{2} </m></p></li>
                    <li><p>Choose which of the 3 non-Joker cards the Jokers will replicate to build the Three of a Kind: <m>\binom{3}{1} </m> options.</p></li>
                </ol>

                    </p>
                   </feedback>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        
    </task>

  
   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->
    <!-- Part E: Final Probability with Wild Jokers (Short Answer Entry) -->
    <task xml:id="task-poker-jokers-prob-final">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Use your answers to parts (a) and (b) to help you determine the probability of being dealt a Three of a Kind hand from the 54-card deck that contains two Jokers. Enter your answer as a decimal rounded to four decimal places.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability  = <fillin  answer="0.0868" width="8"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Make sure to combine counts from the three separate sub-cases: 0, 1, or 2 Jokers.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                    <feedback>
                        <p> Correct!
                Sum the number of Three of a Kind hands across our three separate sub-cases:
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><p><strong>0 Jokers:</strong> <m>54,912</m> hands (calculated in the previous problem)</p></li>
                    <li><p><strong>1 Joker:</strong> <m>164,736</m> hands (calculated in part a)</p></li>
                    <li><p><strong>2 Jokers:</strong> <m>54,912</m> hands (calculated in Part b)</p></li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                Total Three of a Kinds <m>= 54,912 + 164,736 + 54,912 = 274,560</m>  hands.
                </p>
<p>                Divide this  by the total number of possible 5-card hands:  expanded 54-card sample space:
                <m>\binom{54}{5}=3,162,510</m>

                        </p>
                    </feedback>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
 
    </task>
</exercise>


    <!-- Visual Divider ############################################################################################### -->
   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->

<exercise xml:id="counting-harder-student-org-grant-assignments">
    <title>Campus Grant Distribution</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Five student organizations  submit applications for three distinct campus funding awards: the Sustainability Grant, the Diversity Grant, and the Innovation Grant. Each grant must be awarded to a single organization, and no organization can receive more than one grant.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part 1: Fill-In-The-Blank Numeric Input -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                How many distinct outcomes are possible for assigning these three unique grants to the five student organizations? Enter your answer as a whole number.
            </p>
    <p>
      <fillin width="8" answer="60"/>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <evaluation>
    <evaluate>
      <test correct="yes">
        <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        <feedback>
          <p>
Correct! 
                Because the grants are different and an organization can win at most one, this is a permutation problem where we select 3 organizations out of a pool of 5:
                <me>P(5,3) = \frac{5!}{(5-3)!} = 5 \times 4 \times 3 = 60 \text{ outcomes}</me>

          </p>
        </feedback>
      </test>
</evaluate>
</evaluation>

        <hint>
            <p>
                Does order matter?
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Because the grants are different and an organization can win at most one, this is a permutation problem where we arrange 3 organizations out of a pool of 5:
                <me>P(5,3) = \frac{5!}{(5-3)!} = 5 \times 4 \times 3 = 60 \text{ outcomes}</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->
    <!-- Part 2: Multiple Choice Input -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Under the assumption that all  outcomes are equally likely, what is the probability that Organization 1  is awarded one of the grants?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <m>0.6</m>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Correct! If Organization 1 wins a specific grant, then there are <m>_4P_2 = 12</m> ways to assign the remaining two grants to the other four organizations. Multiplying by 3, because there are 3 possible grants Organization 1 could win, gives us 36 favorable outcomes. Divide this by the total number of outcomes to get the specified probability.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <m>0.2</m>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. This is the probability that Organization 1 wins a single, specific grant. Remember that there are 3 different grants available.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <m>0.5</m>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect.  You must count the distinct successful combinations where Organization 1 occupies one of the three grant slots.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <m>0.3</m>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect.  You must count the distinct successful combinations where Organization 1 occupies one of the three grant slots.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <m>0.4</m>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. This is the probability that Organization 1 does not win any of the grant.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>

        <!--<solution>
            <p>
                To find the probability that <m>O_1</m> receives a grant, we determine the number of favorable outcomes and divide by the total sample space:
            </p>
            <p>
                1. Suppose <m>O_1</m> is assigned to a specific grant. There are <m>4</m> remaining organizations left to fill the other <m>2</m> unique grants, which can happen in:
                <me>P(4,2) = 4 \times 3 = 12 \text{ ways}</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                2. Since there are <m>3</m> different grants that <m>O_1</m> could possibly win, we multiply those permutations by 3:
                <me>\text{Favorable Outcomes} = 3 \times 12 = 36 \text{ ways}</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                3. Divide by the total sample space found in Part 1 to compute the final probability:
                <me>P(O_1 \text{ wins a grant}) = \frac{36}{60} = 0.6</me>
            </p>
        </solution> -->

    </task>
</exercise>


