   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->

<exercise label="counting-harder-hackathon-partition">
    <title>Hackathon Team Formations</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            A class of <m>15</m> computer science students is collaborating on a major weekend hackathon project. They decide to split up into two development teams of exactly <m>7</m> students each to work on separate microservices, leaving <m>1</m> remaining student to act as the overall Project Manager. 
        </p>
        <p>
            The two development teams are completely identical in function and do not have specific names or distinct track assignments. How many  ways can the students be divided up? Enter your answer as a whole number.
            </p>
    <p>
      <fillin width="8" answer="25740"/>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <evaluation>
    <evaluate>
      <test correct="yes">
        <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! This problem requires finding the number of ways to partition the 15 students into groups of sizes 7, 7, and 1. We can use the multinomial coefficient for this:
        </p>
        <p>
            <me>\binom{15}{7\  7\  1} = \frac{15!}{7! \cdot 7! \cdot 1!} = 51,480 \text{ ways}</me>
        </p>
        <p>
            Then you have to account for the identical team structures. Because the two  teams are completely indistinguishable, swapping the entire roster of one team with the other does not create a new assignment. So, we must divide by <m>2! = 2</m> to eliminate over counting.
        </p>

        </feedback>
      </test>
</evaluate>
</evaluation>
       

    <hint>
        <p>
            Don't forget to account for the identical team structures.
        </p>
    </hint>

</exercise>


    <!-- Visual Divider ############################################################################################### -->
   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->

<exercise label="ex-multinomial-die-rolls" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Dice Frequencies</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            We have two dice, a fair 6-sided die and a fair 10-sided die. The six sided die is rolled 14 times in a row. The 10-sided die is rolled 8 times in a row. Each individual roll is completely independent of the others. 
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Combinatorial Setup (Dropdown Selection) -->
    <task xml:id="task-die-setup-mc">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the 6-sided die only. Which of the following expressions correctly counts the number of  14-roll sequences  containing exactly 4 ones and 4 twos?
            </p>
            
        </statement>
        <choices randomize ="yes">
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m>\binom{14}{4}\binom{10}{4}4^6</m></p></statement>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>Correct! First, choose 4 positions out of the 14 available slots for the ones: <m>\binom{14}{4}</m>. Next, choose 4 positions out of the remaining 10 vacant slots for the twos: <m>\binom{10}{4}</m>. Then fill the remaining 6 slots with any of the 4 other die numbers (3, 4, 5, or 6), which gives <m>4^6</m> possibilities. Multiplying these together gives the total number of sequences.</p>
                    </feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><m>\binom{14}{4}\binom{14}{4}4^6</m></p></statement>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>Incorrect. After placing the 4 ones (or twos), there are only 10 available slots left in the sequence, not 14.</p>
                    </feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><m>\binom{14}{4}\binom{10}{4}6^6</m></p></statement>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>Incorrect. The remaining 6 positions can only be filled by numbers <em>other than</em> 1 and 2. This leaves exactly 4 options (3, 4, 5, and 6) for each remaining slot.</p>
                    </feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><m>\binom{14}{4} + \binom{10}{4} + 4^6</m></p></statement>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>Incorrect. By the Multiplication Principle,  the components of the answer  must be multiplied together rather than added.</p>
                    </feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><m>6^{14}</m></p></statement>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>Incorrect. This counts the size of the sample space.</p>
                    </feedback></choice>
            </choices>
    </task>


   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->
    <!-- Part B: Numerical Probability (Short Answer Entry) -->
    <task xml:id="task-die-prob-input">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Considering just the 6-sided die again. Compute the probability that the 14 rolls result in exactly 4 ones and exactly 4 twos. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to 4 decimal places.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin  answer="0.0110" width="8"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>
                             Correct! To compute the probability, divide the number of successful sequence arrangements from part (a) by the total size of the  sample space:
<m> 6^{14} </m>.

                        </p>
                    </feedback>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>

    </task>



   <!-- ############################################################################################################## -->
     <task xml:id="task-die-prob-input2">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Now consider just the 10-sided die. Compute the probability that its 8 rolls result in 2 nines being rolled, while the rest are numbers less than or equal to seven. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to 4 decimal places.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability  = <fillin  answer="0.0329" width="8"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                    <feedback>
                        <p> Correct! First, choose 2 positions out of the 8 available slots for the nines.  Then fill the remaining 6 slots with numbers less than or equal to seven, which gives us <m>7^6</m> possibilities. Thus, the probability is:
                                            <me>\frac{\binom{8}{2}7^6}{10^{8}} </me>

                        </p>
                    </feedback>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>

    </task>
</exercise>



   </exercises>



