<exercise label="counting-harder-housing-multinomial-prob">

<title>Campus Housing Room Assignments</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Refer to the problem above with the same name. </p>
            
            <p>A group of <m>12</m> college students are selected to live on a specialized floor in a residence hall. The floor has exactly <m>6</m> rooms available that must be filled: one triple room (<m>3</m> students), three double rooms (<m>2</m> students each), and three single rooms (<m>1</m> student each). 
        </p>
        <p>
            
                Suppose two of the students, Alex and Jordan, are best friends. What is the probability that they are assigned to room together in the same room? Recall: The housing department assigns the <m>12</m> students completely at random to fill the rooms.
            </p>
        </statement>
       
       
      <!--  <solution>
            <p>
                To find the probability, we look at the two distinct scenarios where Alex and Jordan share a room:
            </p>
            <p>
                1. <term>Case 1: They share the Triple Room.</term> Two of the three spots are locked down by Alex and Jordan. The remaining <m>10</m> students must be distributed into the 1 leftover spot in the triple, the three double rooms, and the three single rooms. This yields:
                <me>\frac{10!}{1! \times 2! \times 2! \times 2! \times 1! \times 1! \times 1!} = 453,600 \text{ ways}</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                2. <term>Case 2: They share a Double Room.</term> There are 3 distinct double rooms they could share. If they take over one specific double room, it becomes completely filled. The remaining <m>10</m> students must fill the triple room, the other 2 double rooms, and the three single rooms:
                <me>\frac{10!}{3! \times 2! \times 2! \times 1! \times 1! \times 1!} = 151,200 \text{ ways per room}</me>
                Multiplying by the <m>3</m> optional double rooms gives: <m>3 \times 151,200 = 453,600</m> ways.
            </p>
            <p>
                3. <term>Combine and Calculate Probability:</term> Adding the favorable layouts gives <m>453,600 + 453,600 = 907,200</m> successful configurations. Dividing by our total from Part 1:
                <me>P(\text{Rooming Together}) = \frac{907,200}{1,663,200} = \frac{6}{11} \approx 0.5455</me>
            </p>
        </solution> -->

       
</exercise>




